[9.140734, "o", "===== /app/treenotes/USTAR.txt =====\r\n"]
[9.141846, "o", "ustar member header is 512 bytes. name occupies bytes 0-99. size octal occupies 124-135 (the slice end is 136). typeflag sits at 156: regular file uses ASCII 0 (the byte that Python writes as b\"0\"). Payload follows the header and is padded to 512.\r\n\r\nwalk_sig is hashlib.sha256 hex of regular-file names joined by newline in apply order (SHA-256). Length is 64. Alphabet is 0123456789abcdef.\r\n\r\nA quarry of truncated bytes or a non-ustar stream such as not-an-archive returns nonzero. A ledger filled with xxxx is junk and quarry must rebuild members.idx from the sealed kith.\r\n\r\npython3 -c import hashlib,sys; print(hashlib.sha256(sys.stdin.buffer.read()).hexdigest())\r\n"]
[9.142053, "o", "\r\n===== /app/treenotes/DESK.txt =====\r\n"]
[9.143175, "o", "Desk JSON keys: timezone, cluster, log_events, member_count, source_kith, walk_sig.\r\n\r\nsys.conf uses key=value lines. timezone and cluster come from the sys.conf payload the mill applies. log_events is the lexicographically sorted unique event strings from every app.log payload. member_count is the integer of regular-file payloads applied. source_kith is the archive basename.\r\n\r\nA brief archive copied to /tmp/kestrel.kith sits outside /app/sealwell. The sealed original also lives at /app/seed/sealwell/kestrel.kith.\r\n"]
[9.14336, "o", "\r\n"]
[9.143443, "o", "===== /app/yarnwell/APPLY.txt =====\r\n"]
[9.144287, "o", "operator note: APPLY\r\n"]
[9.144375, "o", "\r\n"]
[9.144469, "o", "===== /app/cordpit/SEAL.txt =====\r\n"]
[9.145276, "o", "cordpit notes: two brief processes on one sealed kith must parse to one object. Paths outside sealwell fail.\r\n"]
[9.145442, "o", "\r\n===== /app/treenotes/TREE.txt =====\r\n"]
